the federal reserve will begin raising  interest rates in march it's a departure  from pandemic era policies that have  been in place since march 2020 pegging  the benchmark rate to zero fed chairman  jerome powell says the move is designed  to temper economic growth americans have  seen decades high inflation rates lately  eating into wage gains and household  budgets the fed chairman  added that he thinks there is quite a  bit of room to raise rates without  threatening the labor market  well i would say  well to help us make sense of all this  we've got stephen beardsley with us from  dw business steve investors around the  world have been eagerly awaiting this  decision from the fed  why is it so important  well it's important because let's start  with the domestic economy right hiking  rates essentially makes borrowing more  expensive when that happens that cuts  into corporate profits and also cuts  into loans that means mortgages car  loans credit cards things like that that  brings down consumer spending so overall  it takes steam out of the american  economy the american economy is the  world's biggest economy and it spills  well out over its borders that means  that any time that economy contracts a  little bit you're going to see a  reaction across the globe now there's  two other things to consider one is that  for exporters into the u.s this could be  good for them because the u.s dollar  should get higher in relation to their  currency that's a good thing for them  but for emerging markets it could be a  bad thing one reason is that many peg  their debt to u.s dollars if the price  of that debt then goes up they have more  to pay back  another reason is that um many have um  they've seen that um what happens is  that when  borrowing rates in the u.s get more  attractive i should say lending rates  you buy a bond for example you're  getting paid more a higher interest rate  for it that money then pulls out of  emerging markets and so that's what  they're going to fear if you're in india  if you're south africa if you're brazil  you're going to be watching closely okay  so there are global implications to this  but  it's really just correcting something in  a situation where there's very high  inflation what does this mean  really how big of a shift is it well in  many ways the fed has been telegraphing  this already for weeks you know there  was some expectation that it could be a  sudden interest rate change that didn't  happen there was an expectation that  perhaps would be a half a percent change  it's only going to be a quarter percent  change most likely so for the most part  markets had already factored a lot of  this in but as you mentioned this is a  shift from two years of a much looser  monetary policy and this is the u.s  basically saying the dynamic has changed  we are now moving on to a post-covet  economy but there are still a lot of  questions that are making markets a  little jittery how many rate cuts are  there going to be how bad is inflation  going to be what's going to curb it so  the dynamic has changed but not everyone  is completely sure what that dynamic  looks like from now on  steve thank you so much steve bearsley  from dw business  you
 

